  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given a binary string AAA of length NNN.\n\nYou can perform the following type of operation on the string AAA:\n\n\nChoose two **different** indices iii and jjj (1≤i,j≤N)(1\\leq i,j\\leq N)(1≤i,j≤N);\nChange AiA_iAi​ and AjA_jAj​ to Ai⊕AjA_i \\oplus A_jAi​⊕Aj​. Here ⊕\\oplus⊕ represents the bitwise XOR operation.\nFind the **minimum** number of operations required to convert the given string into a palindrome.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nFirst line of the input contains TTT, the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nFirst line of each test case contains an integer NNN denoting the size of the string.\nSecond line of each test case contains a binary string AAA of length NNN containing 000s and 111s only.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, print the **minimum** number of operations required to make the string a palindrome.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "1≤N≤2⋅105 1 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceeds 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n5\n11011\n7\n0111010\n1\n1\n4\n1100",
        "output": "0\n1\n0\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 111 :  The given string 110111101111011 is already a palindrome. So, no operation is required. The answer for the case is 000.\nTest Case 222 : The given string 011101001110100111010 is not a palindrome.\n\nChoose the indices i=3i=3i=3 and j=5j=5j=5. Now, A3⊕A5=1⊕0=1A_3 \\oplus A_5 = 1\\oplus 0 = 1A3​⊕A5​=1⊕0=1. Thus, we set A3A_3A3​ and A5A_5A5​ equal to 111.\n\nAfter the operation, the resulting string is 011111001111100111110 which is a palindrome. The number of operations required is 111.\nTest Case 333 :  The given string 111 is already a palindrome. So, no operation is required. The answer for the case is 000.\nTest Case 444 : The given string 110011001100 is not a palindrome.\n\nChoose the indices i=1i=1i=1 and j=2j=2j=2. Now, A1⊕A2=1⊕1=0A_1 \\oplus A_2 = 1\\oplus 1 = 0A1​⊕A2​=1⊕1=0. Thus, we set A1A_1A1​ and A2A_2A2​ equal to 000.\n\nAfter the operation, the resulting string is 000000000000 which is a palindrome. The number of operations required is 111."
      }
    ]
  },
